   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Although Simpson’s Paradox has been known for over a century and has a straightforward probabilistic analysis, we predict that it will remain a source of fruitful philosophical discussion. Pearl’s causal analysis of the paradox is relatively recent, and it is only now that graphical causal models are starting to play a central role in philosophical discussions of the paradox. Despite the continuity between graphical accounts and earlier probabilistic theories of causality, here we have highlight",
   "md_text": "Although Simpson’s Paradox has been known for over a century and has a straightforward probabilistic analysis, we predict that it will remain a source of fruitful philosophical discussion. Pearl’s causal analysis of the paradox is relatively recent, and it is only now that graphical causal models are starting to play a central role in philosophical discussions of the paradox. Despite the continuity between graphical accounts and earlier probabilistic theories of causality, here we have highlight"
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "For matters of exposition, we assume that these frequencies are unbiased estimates of the underlying probabilities. The treatment looks ineffective at the level of the overall population, but it leads to higher success percentages than the control both for men and for women (61% vs. 57% for men and 44% vs. 40% for women). Writing these proportions as conditional probabilities, with ⟦M⟧ =treatment, ⟦M⟧ =success/recovery, and ⟦M⟧ =male subpopulation, we obtain",
   "md_text": "For matters of exposition, we assume that these frequencies are unbiased estimates of the underlying probabilities. The treatment looks ineffective at the level of the overall population, but it leads to higher success percentages than the control both for men and for women (61% vs. 57% for men and 44% vs. 40% for women). Writing these proportions as conditional probabilities, with ⟦M⟧ =treatment, ⟦M⟧ =success/recovery, and ⟦M⟧ =male subpopulation, we obtain"
